---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE, withBase } from '../../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Scope, method, what verification covers, and the limits of this catalog.">
	<div class="prose stack">
		<h1>About</h1>
		<p class="lede">{SITE.description}</p>

		<h2>Scope</h2>
		<p>
			The catalog covers open kidney research datasets: renal tumors, segmentation, chronic and acute kidney
			disease, stones, cysts, transplantation, dialysis and kidney outcomes, across CT, MRI, ultrasound,
			pathology, clinical tables and molecular data. Coverage is deliberately narrow and deep rather than
			exhaustive. This is not the first kidney dataset catalog and does not claim to list every dataset.
		</p>

		<h2>What gets published</h2>
		<p>A dataset release appears here only when all of the following hold, each with a source:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>It is available by direct download or basic registration, without an approval committee.</li>
			<li>Human data have authoritative documentation of de-identification from the provider.</li>
			<li>This site collects and hosts no patient records.</li>
			<li>The provider's terms permit the use made of the material here.</li>
			<li>Every preview image shown has separately checked reuse permission and attribution.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Public and restricted parts of a collection are judged separately. Anything whose eligibility is unclear
			stays unpublished rather than appearing with an "open" label.
		</p>

		<h2>De-identification is not an access level</h2>
		<p>
			HIPAA is a law, not a credential a dataset can hold. Nothing here is described as "HIPAA-free", and being
			downloadable is not evidence that data were de-identified. Where a provider documents de-identification,
			the record says so and cites where it says it. Licences and institutional obligations can still apply to
			de-identified data.
		</p>

		<h2>What verification covers</h2>
		<p>
			A person, not an automated agent, approves each release before it is published. That approval covers the
			five checks above, the reuse permission for every preview shown, and a check of the headline counts
			against their sources. It does not mean every field has been independently reproduced: other fields carry
			the source and the place within it, so you can check them yourself.
		</p>
		<p>
			Each record shows when it was last verified and who approved it. Records the curator has not approved are
			not published, and do not appear in the catalog, search, or exports.
		</p>

		<h2>How unknowns are written</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>No</strong> — the source states this is absent.</li>
			<li><strong>Not documented</strong> — the source does not say.</li>
			<li><strong>Not verified</strong> — a claim exists but has not been checked here.</li>
			<li><strong>Not applicable</strong> — the question does not apply to this release.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			An unknown count is never shown as zero, and unknown is never turned into "no" when releases are compared.
			Counts of patients, examinations, lesions, slices, slides and records are kept apart and never added
			together across overlapping collections.
		</p>

		<h2>Limits</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Dictionary search covers documented variables only. Where a dictionary is partial, the page says so,
				and a variable missing from search may still exist in the dataset.
			</li>
			<li>
				An empty list of quality findings means nothing has been inspected, not that the data are free of
				errors.
			</li>
			<li>Results from different papers are not scored against each other, because their evaluations differ.</li>
			<li>This site grants no access to any dataset and cannot give permission on a provider's behalf.</li>
		</ul>

		<p>
			Found something wrong? <a href={withBase('/contribute/')}>Send a correction</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
